"""Manually reasoned claims. The helper only supplies repeated provenance fields."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
items={x['id']:x for x in json.loads(Path('agents/p15/items_partial.json').read_text())}
C=[]
def c(num,it,loc,statement,onpage,cls,fate,refs,comp='No numerical computation. See computations.md for any formal verification.',confidence='high',words=None,date=None):
 ids=['SA07-'+str(i) for i in it]
 base=items[ids[0]]
 C.append(dict(id=f'CL-SA07-{num:03}',items=ids,cote=loc.split('/')[0],scans=loc.split(' L')[0].split('/',1)[1],lines=loc.split(' L')[-1],his_statement=words or base['his_words'],statement=statement,status_on_pages=onpage,computation=comp,**{'class':cls},fate=fate,fate_date=date or 'Dates and first circulation are separated in the cited reference records.',present_status='As of 2026-11-19: '+fate,refs=refs,confidence=confidence))

c(1,[4],'15/195 L15–19','For a characteristic-p point with perfect residue field k and a characteristic-zero point, compare the indicated crystalline fibre with the integral p-adic fibre extended to W(k). The author tentatively expects nonisomorphism in general but retains a qualified isomorphism assertion when k is finite; the final qualifier is uncertain. The tensor, integral, Frobenius and filtration structures to be preserved are not fully specified.','Open on the pages; tentative wording, local deletions and an uncertain live finite-field exception.','C','Comparison theorems relate specified realizations after extending coefficients, but do not identify arbitrary integral or filtered fibre functors. Neither the general expectation nor the uncertain finite-field exception can be matched to one theorem with the manuscript’s unspecified category.',['P15-D82','P15-CF00'],confidence='medium')
c(2,[5],'15/195 L20–25','A characteristic-zero de Rham realization should carry both weight and Hodge filtrations, with the indicated covariance under change of point.','Construction proposed without complete hypotheses.','B','Mixed Hodge theory supplies compatible weight and Hodge filtrations for complex algebraic varieties. It does not, alone, prove the manuscript’s universal motivic essential-image claim.',['P15-D71'])
c(3,[6],'15/197 L50–53','Automorphisms of the realization field fixing the residue field act semilinearly by descent; an automorphism moving that residue-field embedding instead gives a conjugate functor.','Stated without proof.','E','This is ordinary scalar descent. It distinguishes an action on one object from transport to a different scalar structure; no canonical isomorphism between the latter is implied.',['P15-D82','P15-DM82'],comp='computations.md §D1 gives the scalar-twist verification.')
c(4,[7],'15/197 L53','The difference of two existing neutralizations is measured by a torsor class; a gerbe obstruction to having a neutralization is a different degree of nonabelian cohomology.','The transcription has H^{2|1}; not a determinate exponent.','B','For two fibre functors the comparison object is an Isom torsor, hence H¹ when a band/group is fixed. H² pertains to the prior gerbe obstruction. The ink must decide which assertion this line intended.',['P15-DM82'],confidence='medium')
c(5,[8],'15/197–199 L53–79','For a rational algebraic point, no canonical identification of its de Rham realization over Q_l with the étale Q_l realization is predicted merely from their existence; integral origins can differ.','Stated as a warning and non-isomorphism expectation.','C','Betti–étale and complex de Rham comparisons are established, and p-adic comparison requires period rings and hypotheses. These do not supply a bare canonical Q_l isomorphism. The sweeping non-isomorphism expectation is not a theorem in this generality.',['P15-D82','P15-CF00'])
c(6,[9],'15/199 L89–96','For a complex algebraic point, the integral Betti realization tensored with Z_l agrees with the étale realization, in a cohomological degree and torsion convention where both are defined.','Stated without proof.','B','The comparison theorem is established; the opened Deligne account explicitly points to SGA4 XI. The manuscript’s free-lattice tensor-category packaging still needs its torsion convention.',['P15-D82'],words='ξ_{x,Z}⊗Z_l ≃ ξ_{x,l}')
c(7,[9],'15/199 L89–96','Complexifying Betti cohomology gives algebraic de Rham cohomology for smooth complex algebraic varieties, functorially.','Stated without proof.','A','Grothendieck’s de Rham comparison theorem proves the cohomological statement. The 1966 paper records its1963 circulation separately.',['P15-G66'],words='ξ_{x,Z}⊗C ≃ ξ_{x,∞}')
c(8,[9,10],'15/199–201 L96–132','Complex conjugation canonically identifies the integral topological realizations of a complex point and its conjugate; over a real point this gives an involution.','Stated without proof, with a deleted alternative formulation.','E','Complex conjugation is an involutive homeomorphism between the two analytic spaces. Its pullback on integral cohomology is involutive and functorial; the real-point specialization is an endomorphism.',['P15-D82'],comp='computations.md §D2 distinguishes topological pullback and coefficient conjugation.')
c(9,[10],'15/203 L162–184','In the real geometric situation the two semilinear conjugations commute and their product is the linear real Frobenius.','Self-flagged twice as à vérifier.','B','The standard real comparison diagram verifies this when the de Rham structure and analytic involution come from the same real variety. Arbitrary semilinear involutions need not commute.',['P15-D82'],comp='C09 in code/compute_checks.py and §D2; counterexample concerns omission of geometric compatibility.')
c(10,[11],'15/203–205 L180–213','Real Frobenius acts linearly on the Betti realization, reverses Hodge bidegrees, and participates in the displayed filtration comparison.','Self-flagged/stated without proof.','B','The comparison is established for a variety over R; exchanging (p,q) follows by conjugating holomorphic and antiholomorphic forms. It is not the scalar conjugation itself.',['P15-D82'],comp='computations.md §D2.')
c(11,[12,13],'15/205 L212–221','The rational intersection of the indicated Hodge-filtration step with its real-Frobenius transform is asserted to be the motivic part of bounded level.','Stated without proof; the index rho and the meaning of level are uncertain.','A','Under the naive ordinary-filtration interpretation this is false: rational intersection need not be a Hodge substructure. Grothendieck’s1969 correction replaces it by the largest sub-Hodge structure in the filtration. The uncertain manuscript notation prevents an unconditional literal refutation of every possible reading.',['P15-G69','P15-D71'],comp='C12 and computations.md §D3 give a dimension5 obstruction and its real descent; no conjecture is tested numerically.',confidence='medium')
c(12,[14],'15/207;225 L253–255;834–836','Different complex places should always give non-isomorphic underlying Betti fibre functors; he subsequently rejects this bare formulation.','Corrected by himself: faux at207, renewed rejection at225.','C','The universal bare-place assertion is withdrawn. Charles proves existence of conjugate varieties with different real cohomology rings, which is weaker than separation of every pair of places or every abstract tensor functor.',['P15-Charles09'],words='faux; sans filtration de Hodge cela est faux')
c(13,[15],'15/207 L259–261','In positive characteristic the whole motivic gerbe may admit no R-valued fibre functor; whether a totally real field neutralizes it is also asked.','Open on the pages.','B','A category containing h¹ of a supersingular elliptic curve and all geometric endomorphisms cannot have a usual two-dimensional real fibre realization: its quaternion endomorphism algebra is nonsplit over R. Thus no totally real neutralizing field exists for that category. A smaller category with fewer endomorphisms requires separate analysis.',['P15-Voight21','P15-DM82'],comp='C04; computations.md §D4 explains faithfulness, dimension2 and the all-endomorphisms hypothesis.')
c(14,[16],'15/207 L267–269','The gerbe of fibre functors and the Isom torsor between two chosen neutralizations encode related but distinct data.','Construction recalled without proof.','B','Tannakian duality makes the distinction precise: choosing a fibre functor gives a group, and other fibre functors form its torsors. A gerbe need not possess a rational object.',['P15-DM82'])
c(15,[17],'15/209 L283–285','Twisting a chosen fibre functor by a torsor gives another fibre functor with the corresponding inner form of its automorphism group.','Stated as a construction.','B','This is the standard contracted-product construction in Tannakian duality. It classifies fibre functors relative to a fixed neutralization, not geometric points of X.',['P15-DM82'],comp='computations.md §D5 gives the descent construction.')
c(16,[18],'15/209 L285–300','Enriched real/complex points should be in bijection with suitably enhanced fibre functors; the proposed enhancements include Hodge data and descent.','Conjectural classification.','C','The torsor classification of fibre functors is established, and integral Hodge structures classify complex abelian varieties in weight1. Neither result classifies all geometric points of an arbitrary variety through its entire proposed motivic category.',['P15-DM82','P15-MilneSV'])
c(17,[19],'15/211 L336–353','A point with characteristic-p/crystalline data should be recoverable from an integral realization endowed with Witt, Frobenius and Verschiebung structure.','Questions à revoir; no proof.','C','Oda identifies the H¹ de Rham/finite p-group data of abelian varieties; p-adic admissibility addresses a different rational representation category. The proposed integral motivic point classification remains unspecified and is not supplied by either theorem.',['P15-Oda69','P15-CF00'])
c(18,[20],'15/211 L354–369','Filtered characteristic-zero realizations should characterize a geometric point by additional conditions yet to be specified.','Open on the pages.','C','Known Hodge and p-adic realization theories give necessary structures and precise special equivalences, not the universal geometric essential image asked for here.',['P15-D71','P15-CF00','P15-MilneSV'])
c(19,[21],'15/213 L406–415','The automorphism group of a fixed integral fibre functor is the group of integral points G_xi(Z); the topological fundamental group acts through a homomorphism into it.','Stated without proof.','B','In a represented integral Tannakian setting Aut^tensor(xi)=G_xi(Z) is definitional. Monodromy gives a homomorphism; identifying its image or closure with the whole motivic group requires further hypotheses.',['P15-DM82','P15-Arapura13'])
c(20,[22],'15/213 L411–415','Only finitely many integral fibre functors should occur for the fixed type alpha, by an appeal to Borel–Harish-Chandra.','Stated without the reduction to the cited theorem.','C','Borel–Harish-Chandra6.9 proves finiteness of arithmetic orbits of integral points on a closed orbit of a rational reductive group. The pages do not identify this moduli problem with such an orbit, prove closedness, or control all integral models. The general finiteness claim therefore remains conditional.',['P15-BHC62','P15-BHC61'])
c(21,[23],'15/213;215 L433–438;475–481','Assuming the appropriate Hodge conjecture, the Hodge realization of the proposed pure algebraic-correspondence motives is fully faithful.','Explicitly conditional.','C','Fullness amounts to algebraicity of the relevant Hodge tensors after duality and twists. The general Hodge conjecture remains the missing premise; abelian/motivated or Nori results do not remove that premise for algebraic correspondences.',['P15-D71','P15-D82','P15-Arapura13'])
c(22,[23],'15/215 L475–481','Assuming Tate for the relevant reductions over finite fields, the proposed arithmetic realization has the fullness/normality needed in the motivic-group argument.','Explicitly conditional.','C','Tate-type realization fullness is known in important special classes, but the stated all-motives premise is not established by the opened sources. The conditional role must be retained rather than replaced by the theorem for abelian-variety Hom groups.',['P15-DM82','P15-Oda69'],confidence='medium')
c(23,[24],'15/213–215 L433–481','Describe the essential image of the Hodge/étale realization, not only its Hom spaces.','Open on the pages.','C','An equivalence for polarizable weight1 integral Hodge structures supplies the abelian case. General motivic essential-image recognition is stronger than full faithfulness and is not settled by the comparison results searched.',['P15-MilneSV','P15-Arapura13','P15-CF00'])
c(24,[26],'15/215 L486–498','After extending the real base field to C, the geometric motivic group is connected/irreducible in the intended category.','Stated conditionally in the surrounding Hodge/Tate discussion.','C','The corrected Deligne–Milne6.22(a)/6.23(a) requires all relevant Hodge cycles to be absolute Hodge for the geometric connectedness identification; the original1982 omission is explicitly repaired. Abelian motives satisfy the needed condition. No unconditional all-algebraic-motives conclusion is licensed.',['P15-DM82'])
c(25,[26],'15/215 L486–498','The real arithmetic group is generated by its identity component and real Frobenius, yielding the desired fullness comparison.','Proof sketch with conditional inputs.','C','Given the exact quotient by Gal(C/R), a lift of its nontrivial element plus the connected geometric kernel generates the group. The group-theoretic argument is formal; identifying that kernel with the identity component uses the preceding hypotheses.',['P15-DM82'],comp='computations.md §D6 proves the group-generation implication.')
c(26,[27],'15/215 L507–509','A morphism of realizations over a connected base which is Hodge/motivic at one fibre should be so everywhere.','Stated without proof.','B','Deligne4.1.3.1 proves fixed Hodge type for a global flat section in a smooth proper algebraic family. For abelian schemes4.1.3.2 proves extension of a global integral local-system morphism that is algebraic at one fibre. General algebraic-cycle realization requires extra conjectures.',['P15-D71'])
c(27,[28],'15/217 L556–561','A morphism to a fine moduli space should be determined by its value at one point and induced topological monodromy data.','Claim with abbreviated justification.','C','For moduli of polarized abelian varieties with sufficient level, the H¹ local system together with one Hodge fibre and polarization determines the family via fixed-part rigidity and relative Hodge equivalence. The pages do not state an arbitrary-moduli theorem.',['P15-D71','P15-MilneSV'],comp='computations.md §D7 states the restricted proof and its level/polarization hypotheses.')
c(28,[29],'15/217 L563–569','Characterize maps to the moduli space by group homomorphisms and point data satisfying suitable Hodge conditions.','Inverse problem left open.','C','The weight1 relative Hodge equivalence gives a precise restricted recognition theorem once a polarized integral variation of the correct type is supplied. An arbitrary abstract group homomorphism plus a point need not produce such a variation.',['P15-MilneSV','P15-D71'])
c(29,[30],'15/217 L573–579','A motivically pointed variety should support a reconstruction formalism for morphisms and sections.','Programmatic construction.','C','Modern motivic local systems and Tannakian formalism make some of this structure precise, but the manuscript does not specify a category in which every scheme is recovered. No universal recognition theorem matching this wording was found.',['P15-Arapura13','P15-DM82'])
c(30,[31],'15/219 L621–674','Isomorphism of all finite pieces of two fibre functors should lead to an isomorphism of the full functors.','Finite-stage proof followed by a separate limit problem.','C','Finite-stage nonemptiness alone is insufficient: one needs compatible choices or a compactness/limit theorem. The manuscript supplies a countable path-tower argument but leaves the uncountable stage unfinished. General torsor theory does not silently supply a rational point.',['P15-DM82','P15-Kahn25'],comp='computations.md §D8 separates finite-stage and inverse-limit assertions.')
c(31,[32],'15/219 L621–674','For a normal irreducible variety over a field relatively algebraically closed in its function field, finite collections of algebraic data spread to geometrically connected models, allowing Betti comparison along complex paths under the stated agreement of embeddings.','Proof sketched.','A','Finite-presentation descent and geometric connectedness arguments are part of EGA’s limit machinery. They justify finite-stage spreading, not arbitrary simultaneous infinite choices. Exact primary EGA8.8.2 verification is recorded separately in the source ledger.',['P15-G66','P15-DM82'],comp='computations.md §D8; EGA citation to be added after original-page verification.',confidence='medium')
c(32,[33],'15/221 L720–734','For a countable nested sequence of path sets with nonempty terms and surjective transition maps, a compatible path exists.','Proof sketched by recursive choice.','E','Countable dependent choice constructs a point of the inverse limit. The mathematical issue is verifying the hypotheses for the stated models, not the recursive argument.',['P15-DM82'],comp='computations.md §D8 gives the exact induction; no finite computation is presented as a proof.')
c(33,[34],'15/221 L736–738','Extend the path comparison construction to uncountable coefficient fields.','Unfinished on the pages.','F','The pages do not state the required inverse system, topology or compactness property. The countable proof does not establish this extension. Searches of EGA limits and Tannakian descent identify tools, not a theorem matching the incomplete sentence.',['P15-DM82','P15-Kahn25'],confidence='low')
c(34,[35],'15/221 L738–746','The algebraic part E of the field should control separation of embeddings by Betti fibre functors, with a hoped-for distinction for embeddings differing on E.','Expectation, no proof.','C','Finite étale data recover algebraic Galois structure, but separation by the full unadorned Betti fibre functor is a different assertion. The later counterexamples prove existence of some distinctions, not this universal quantified claim.',['P15-Charles09','P15-DM82'])
c(35,[36],'15/223 L795–796','There should exist smooth projective conjugate varieties with non-isomorphic integral cohomology rings, possibly even over R.','Question/conjecture.','B','Charles constructs smooth projective conjugates over a number field with non-isomorphic graded real cohomology algebras. This also rules out isomorphism of their integral cohomology rings. First preprint2007, publication2009.',['P15-Charles09'])
c(36,[37],'15/223 L799–804','For a sufficiently large motivic category, a complex place should be recoverable from the associated comparison torsor.','Conjecture with vague sufficiently-large hypothesis.','C','Tannakian theory classifies comparison torsors once a category is fixed. It does not prove injectivity of the geometric-place map; Hodge-enhanced special cases and period genericity are distinct additional inputs.',['P15-DM82','P15-BC16'])
c(37,[38],'15/223 L799–804','Every relevant torsor might come from a complex place; the pages themselves raise Riemann–Hodge restrictions.','Open question, with warning.','C','Arbitrary tensor torsors need not carry effective polarizable integral Hodge structures of geometric origin. Weight1 gives a precise restricted image; the universal surjectivity claim is neither formulated with these conditions nor established.',['P15-MilneSV','P15-DM82'])
c(38,[39],'15/223 L807–810','The abstract motivic groups associated to different places might themselves fail to be isomorphic.','A priori expectation, not theorem.','F','A nontrivial comparison torsor can twist a group while leaving its abstract isomorphism class unchanged. No specific category/group/pair is given to turn this expectation into a testable statement.',['P15-DM82'],confidence='medium')
c(39,[40],'15/225 L834–850','Adding the Hodge filtration may restore separation of places that fails for bare integral fibre functors.','Question proposed after explicit withdrawal.','C','For elliptic curves the integral Hodge structure determines the complex elliptic curve and hence j. Recovering an arbitrary field embedding additionally needs a sufficiently rich functorial family and distinguishes an embedding from its conjugacy class.',['P15-MilneSV'],comp='C01 and computations.md §D9 prove the conditional elliptic-family test; the explicit family belongs to the earlier run.')
c(40,[41],'15/225 L849–850','The elliptic moduli parameter j on a punctured line provides a test for the enriched-place question.','Example/strategy, no full proof.','B','The j-invariant classifies complex elliptic curves up to isomorphism, and integral Hodge reconstruction recovers it. Rational Hodge structures give only isogeny and do not suffice for this exact classification.',['P15-MilneSV'],comp='C01 and §D9; excluded parameters and characteristic zero explicit.')
c(41,[42],'15/227 L877–879','Determine when tensor functors between motivic categories arise from morphisms of varieties.','Open recognition question.','C','Modern Nori/Ayoub comparison identifies certain motivic Galois constructions, not full reconstruction of arbitrary varieties from tensor functors. Additional geometric and realization constraints remain.',['P15-CG17','P15-Arapura13'])
c(42,[43],'15/227 L879–880','An immersion into suitable moduli spaces should supply a criterion for an anabelian property in the field case.','Tentative and abbreviated.','F','The precise anabelian category, morphisms, and immersion condition are not specified. Weight1 moduli rigidity is a nearby established result, not a proof of this incomplete criterion.',['P15-MilneSV','P15-D71'],confidence='low')
c(43,[44],'15/225 L838','The local insertion proposes an extra restriction related to one of the fibre-functor reconstruction claims.','Illegible/uncertain insertion; no identifiable full statement.','F','No determinate mathematical proposition can be recovered without deciding the ink; the local classification sources do not disambiguate it.',['P15-DM82'],confidence='low')
c(44,[45],'18/005 L950–964','List six global realizations: motivic, Hodge/Betti, de Rham, l-adic and related integral/rational variants, with the indicated arrows.','Programmatic diagram, not six proved equivalences.','C','The constituent comparison functors are established in specified categories; universal equivalences and essential images do not follow. Nori/Ayoub theory and Hodge/p-adic comparisons settle different arrows under different hypotheses.',['P15-D82','P15-CG17','P15-CF00'])
c(45,[46,48,49],'18/005 L966–976','For a p-divisible group up to isogeny over a perfect residue field/local field, ask for the missing analogues1′ and4′ linking crystalline, étale and filtered data.','Two numbered gaps explicitly left.','C','Oda proves a precise abelian H¹/Dieudonné result; Colmez–Fontaine gives the rational semistable filtered-module equivalence. Neither classifies integral p-divisible groups without weight/lattice conditions. The l-adic versus p-adic letter must be checked on the scan.',['P15-Oda69','P15-CF00'])
c(46,[47],'18/005 L972–976','Characterize the image of the filtered Frobenius realization in the intended local category.','Open essential-image problem.','B','In the rational semistable setting, weak admissibility is sufficient by Colmez–Fontaine; N=0 is the crystalline case. This resolves that precise later formulation, while the manuscript’s integral p-divisible/motivic formulation needs narrower weights and lattices.',['P15-CF00'])
c(47,[50,76],'18/006 L989–1008','Modify two sections of an extension by homomorphisms into the centralizing kernel, producing new homomorphic sections.','Computation/sketch on the pages.','E','A product of a section and a kernel-valued homomorphism is a section homomorphism when their images commute. This is elementary group theory; equality of the two resulting sections is a separate condition.',['P15-DM82'],comp='C05 and computations.md §D10 give the multiplication formula.')
c(48,[76],'18/006 L1001–1008','The two modified sections coincide.','Asserted after the centralization argument without the compatibility equality.','F','Centralization does not imply coincidence. The direct-product example gives distinct homomorphic sections satisfying the displayed type of assumptions. An additional compatibility relation, if intended elsewhere, must be supplied.',['P15-DM82'],comp='C05 and §D10; refined true assertion is CL-SA07-047.')
c(49,[50,93],'18/006 L1012–1022','If u and j composed with chi are homomorphisms with commuting images, phi=u(j chi)^(-1) is a homomorphism.','Computation sketched.','E','The multiplication identity proves the claim under the stated commuting-images hypothesis. Removing it invalidates the argument.',['P15-DM82'],comp='computations.md §D10 gives the symbolic group calculation.')
c(50,[50,93],'18/006 L1012–1022','The manuscript identifies ker(u) with ker(phi) intersect ker(chi).','Stated without the required character premise.','C','One inclusion is automatic. Equality additionally requires chi to be trivial on ker(u), equivalently factor through the image of u. A finite C2 counterexample refutes the implication from the displayed data alone.',['P15-DM82'],comp='C06 and §D10; this is a counterexample to missing premises, not to unseen LM6 statements.')
c(51,[77],'18/006 L1019–1024','The quotient by the kernel of a continuous p-adic linear representation is a p-adic analytic group.','Stated/sketched.','B','For a profinite source and a continuous representation into a finite-dimensional p-adic Lie group, the image is compact hence closed; closed-subgroup theory makes the quotient p-adic analytic. Without continuity, compactness or finite-dimensional target, the wording is too broad.',['P15-Gl16'],comp='computations.md §D11 supplies the topological quotient argument.')
c(52,[51],'18/007 L1029–1041','Specialization of K-theoretic cycle classes commutes with l-adic cohomology after rationalization.','Published-text draft with explicit reference SGA6X7.13.11.','A','SGA6X7.13.7–10 proves rationalized compatibility using the Chern character; the original printed576–579 was viewed. This does not retain torsion.',['P15-SGA6'])
c(53,[51,52],'18/007 L1029–1041','For smooth proper X over a trait, specialization of raw cycles is compatible with integral l-adic cycle classes; passage to Chow groups needs compatibility with rational equivalence.','Claim stated; discussion deliberately weakened to il semble.','A','The manuscript matches SGA6X7.13.11–12, but the available original scan omits printed580 containing the integral square. Its start and conclusion were verified on579/581. The exact middle remains a source-access limitation, not evidence that the mathematical statement is false.',['P15-SGA6'],confidence='medium')
c(54,[53],'18/009 L1047–1065','For a Tate twist E(n), the weight index shifts by2n and the Hodge index byn, while complex conjugation on the Tate factor contributes the expected sign.','Definitions/calculation.','B','These are the standard Tate conventions: Q(n) has weight−2n and type(−n,−n). The twist itself must remain in graded formulas.',['P15-D71','P15-D82'],comp='C14 and computations.md §D12.')
c(55,[53],'18/009 L1057','The unshifted expression for the weight-graded piece of E(n) is written in a way that can be read as Gr_i(E)(n).','Mathematical reading alarm, not silently emended.','C','If read literally without the2n shift it fails for E=Q and n nonzero. The consistent formula is Gr_i^W(E(n))=Gr_(i+2n)^W(E)(n). The ink may already contain the needed index.',['P15-D71'],comp='C14; refined statement CL-SA07-054.',confidence='medium')
c(56,[58,88],'18/009;011 L1047–1065;1098–1107','A grading of the identity tensor functor corresponds to a central cocharacter, while a grading of a chosen realization need not be central in the full motivic group.','Mixed conventions developed on the pages.','B','Deligne–MilneDefinition5.1 makes the identity-functor centrality precise. A Hodge splitting cocharacter centralizes its Levi subgroup, not generally all of G.',['P15-DM82'],comp='C08 and §D13; GL2 distinguishes the two centrality assertions.')
c(57,[54],'18/011 L1081–1088','The three tensor-Isom spaces between Betti, de Rham and graded Hodge realizations form a triangle; the comparison points compose cyclically.','Construction with formulas.','B','Composition of tensor isomorphisms gives the contracted-product torsor identities. Domains/codomains and left/right actions must be kept; no algebraic-independence conclusion follows just from composition.',['P15-DM82','P15-BC16'],comp='computations.md §D13 verifies composition types.')
c(58,[54,57],'18/011 L1095–1114','The filtration variety is G/P and the bigrading/splitting variety G/L for a parabolic P=L⋉U, so their dimensions are g−b and g−b+u.','Computation on the pages, with a reversed forgetful arrow.','B','For a reductive group and its parabolic/Levi, dimP=dimL+dimU proves the dimensions. The natural map forgets the splitting: Big→Fil. An arrow in the opposite direction needs a choice.',['P15-DM82'],comp='C08 and computations.md §D13; hypotheses stated explicitly.')
c(59,[55],'18/011 L1098–1107','The object Q is treated both as a space of splittings of dimensionu and as an H′-torsor where H′ includes a Tate G_m of dimensionu+1.','Unresolved dimension/type mismatch.','F','Those are different objects unless a Tate trivialization or quotient has been imposed. The GL2 model exhibits the one-dimensional discrepancy. No source resolves which convention the manuscript intends.',['P15-DM82','P15-BC16'],comp='C08; do not substitute one dimension into the period conjecture without fixing Q.')
c(60,[58],'18/011 L1103–1107','The cocharacter j attached to the chosen Hodge bigrading is central in the stabilizing Levi subgroup and splits the character epsilon there.','Stated with tensor convention.','B','Centrality within the Levi follows because the Levi preserves each graded component; the Tate character condition gives epsilon composed with j=id under the chosen normalization. It is not centrality in all G.',['P15-DM82'],comp='computations.md §D13.')

c(61,[56],'18/011 L1099–1102','The reflex field should be attached to the conjugacy class of the Hodge cocharacter, rather than to a completely chosen splitting point.','The note extrapolates from his notation; no complete reflex-field theorem on this page.','F','The modern reflex-field construction is available for Shimura data, but the line does not fix such a datum or identify the field of its particular splitting. This is primarily an earlier-run interpretation.',['P15-MilneSV'],confidence='medium')
c(62,[59,60,61],'18/011 L1125–1153','The field generated by the period comparison and Hodge splitting should have transcendence degree g+u over the stated ground field; several field-tower equalities are deduced.','Strong conjecture, followed by conditional calculations.','C','The full arithmetic period conjecture is still not proved for a general non-CM elliptic curve in the opened2026 sources. The extra splitting parameter is stronger than the ordinary period claim and depends on the unresolved definition/dimension of Q. Functional transcendence over C is a different relative theorem.',['P15-BC16','P15-KSV26','P15-BT25'],comp='computations.md §D14 derives the tower equalities conditionally and isolates the base-field denominator.')
c(63,[60,61],'18/011 L1131–1153','If the joint field has maximal transcendence degree and the component fields satisfy the stated upper bounds, then the displayed intermediate degrees attain those bounds and are algebraically independent in the required relative sense.','Conditional field calculation.','E','Transcendence-degree additivity proves the implication once all fields lie in a common overfield, the base is correct, and the dimension bounds refer to the same objects. It does not establish the conjectural maximal degree.',['P15-BC16'],comp='computations.md §D14; the first field quotient must read K over k, subject to ink verification.')
c(64,[62],'18/012 L1157–1182','After fixing or dividing out the Tate period, the normalized period matrix should be generic in a group of dimension g−1.','Entire page cancelled.','C','A fixed determinant defines an SL-type slice after the necessary scalar extension. Genericity in that slice remains conjectural in non-CM elliptic examples. The cancelled formulation must not be combined unmarked with the live next page.',['P15-G66','P15-BC16','P15-KSV26'],comp='C15; the square-root extension and fixed determinant are explicit.')
c(65,[64],'18/012 L1161–1173','For a non-CM elliptic curve over an algebraic number field, three suitably normalized periods should be algebraically independent.','Cancelled conjectural example, with Schneider comparison.','C','Schneider-type individual/ratio transcendence does not prove three-variable algebraic independence. The full four-dimensional period conjecture and its determinant-normalized form remain open in the searched primary2026 account.',['P15-G66','P15-KSV26','P15-BC16'],comp='C15 and §D14 distinguish determinant normalization from independence.')
c(66,[65],'18/012 L1173–1179','For transcendental j, the normalized period expression is assigned a total transcendence degree4 after including j.','Cancelled and base-field-dependent calculation.','F','Over Qbar(j) and over Qbar the degrees differ by the transcendence degree of j. Without a precise generic family/base field and normalization this is not one well-defined arithmetic conjecture. The geometric period theorem does not license every specialization.',['P15-BT25','P15-BC16'],comp='computations.md §D14 records the field-tower distinction.',confidence='medium')
c(67,[70],'18/012 L1178–1182','Identify the fields marked preminimal/maximal and the parameter xi or zeta used in the cancelled calculation.','Uncertain symbols and undeveloped terminology.','F','The page does not define these fields sufficiently to match a later theorem. Searches through period-torsor and splitting sources supply nearby constructions, not an identification of this notation.',['P15-BC16','P15-Katz76'],confidence='low')
c(68,[66],'18/013 L1184–1190','For an elliptic curve over Qbar, the full period/quasiperiod field should have transcendence degree4 without CM and2 with CM.','Live conjecture/assertion, distinct from cancelled normalization.','C','The CM value2 is established by the Chudnovsky result as precisely discussed in the opened period literature. The non-CM value4 remains the full arithmetic period conjecture in KSV’s2026 account. The existence of linear or generated-torsor results does not prove it.',['P15-BC16','P15-KSV26'],comp='C15; normalization explains why the cancelled page instead displays3.')
c(69,[66],'18/013 L1184–1190','The Hodge/motivic group for an elliptic curve is GL2 in the non-CM case and the restriction-of-scalars CM torus in the CM case, with the appropriate category and Tate character.','Stated without proof.','B','The Hodge group with similitudes/Mumford–Tate group has these forms. The motive must be taken in the standard abelian setting where the Hodge description applies; the weight-normalized determinant-one group is a different group.',['P15-D82','P15-KSV26'],comp='C08 and C15 distinguish full and normalized dimensions.')
c(70,[63,67],'18/013 L1190–1193','The smallest algebraic group/torsor generated by the elliptic comparison point should equal the motivic one.','Question H=G?.','B','KSVv3 Proposition3.1 together with Theorem5.13 settles the suitable de Rham–Betti generated-torsor formulation for an elliptic curve over Qbar, with coefficient conventions retained. This is weaker than equality of the Zariski locus of the single period point with the full torsor.',['P15-KSV26','P15-BC16'])
c(71,[68],'18/013 L1193–1196','For a non-CM elliptic curve over Qbar, the Hodge splitting point should not be algebraic over Qbar.','Question/expectation.','C','This is the de Rham splitting converse asked by Katz4.0.8 and discussed by Bertrand. No primary proof of its full converse was located in the targeted searches. Algebraicity relative to Betti Qbar and relative to algebraic de Rham Qbar are different conditions; web answers conflating them were excluded.',['P15-Katz76','P15-Bertrand78','P15-BC16','P15-MO-Katz'],confidence='medium')
c(72,[69],'18/013 L1193–1196','For a CM elliptic curve over Qbar with its CM endomorphisms defined, the Hodge splitting is algebraic over Qbar.','Asserted.','B','The two eigenspaces of a nonscalar CM endomorphism give the algebraic splitting; Katz4.0.7 and BDP equation1.4.1 identify it with the complex Hodge decomposition.',['P15-Katz76','P15-BDP13'],comp='computations.md §D15 gives the projector ([a]*−a)/(bar(a)−a).')
c(73,[71],'18/015 L1207–1225','For a smooth complex closed immersion of codimensionp with the manuscript’s residue convention, P_top=(2πi)^(-p)P_an.','Local computation and reduction sketch.','B','The comparison is established with the chosen orientation/residue convention. Modern duality traces can add convention-dependent signs, so this formula is not identified indiscriminately with every trace normalization.',['P15-G66','P15-BC16','P15-D82'],comp='C02 and computations.md §D16; product orientation gives the p-th power.')
c(74,[72],'18/015 L1215–1225','Reduce the codimensionp residue comparison to an isolated transverse intersection.','Sketch contains transversal dimension n−p.','C','For a codimensionp Y in an n-fold, a transversal producing an isolated intersection has complex dimensionp. The written n−p can only fit a different use of n or p; it is a scan-reading alarm, not a source emendation.',['P15-G66'],comp='computations.md §D16: dim(Y∩T)=dimY+dimT−n.')
c(75,[71,74],'18/017 L1260–1275','The analytic residue class dz/z integrates to2πi around the positively oriented unit circle, fixing the codimension-one scalar.','Computation on the pages, with instruction faire le raisonnement.','E','The parametrization z=e^(it),0≤t≤2π proves the scalar directly. Reversing the circle orientation reverses the sign; it must match the topological generator.',['P15-G66'],comp='C02, exact symbolic integral and §D16.')
c(76,[73,74],'18/017 L1280–1284','The residue/Gysin comparison holds globally and for arbitrary cohomology classes, with the diagram’s cohomological shift.','Global assertion; explanation cut off; diagram has i+p.','C','Compatibility with cup products, local fundamental classes and gluing extends the local scalar in the standard purity setting. A codimensionp complex immersion shifts ordinary cohomological degree by2p, so i+p needs an alternative grading or correction. The missing proof is not supplied by the one-circle calculation alone.',['P15-G66','P15-D82'],comp='computations.md §D16 states the required local-to-global argument; no claim that the unfinished manuscript proves it.')

# Reused typed sheets are kept as a separate, non-motivic series of claims.
c(77,[95,108],'15/198 L65–73','For an sl2 highest-weight string in characteristic zero, either all lowering vectors are independent or termination occurs at an integer highest weight q≥0; finite-dimensional representations are completely reducible.','Editorial insertion of missing finite-dimensional hypotheses and requests for references.','E','Highest-weight classification and Weyl’s finite-dimensional complete-reducibility theorem establish the corrected statements. Infinite highest-weight modules are not covered by finite-dimensional semisimplicity.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='C11 and computations.md §D17 derive the string coefficients and termination condition.')
c(78,[108],'15/198 L65–67;15/200 L124','Use divided powers e_n=Y^n e/n! and include stability under binomial(H,n) in the integral lattice exercise.','Explicit normalization and correction.','E','For the integral highest-weight lattice of sl2, divided-power and binomial Cartan operators have integral coefficients. Ordinary Lie-generator stability alone is a weaker requirement.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='C11 and §D17 establish the coefficient formulas; no general Chevalley integral-form theorem is inferred from this single rank-one calculation.')
c(79,[95],'15/198 L73','Replace an allegedly easy argument by a detailed Casimir calculation.','Deferred proof; the actual operator/normalization lies on an absent sheet.','F','The standard Casimir invariant and complete-reducibility proof are verified in Milne§5, but the precise requested calculation cannot be reconstructed from this instruction alone.',['P15-MilneLAG'],confidence='medium')
c(80,[109],'15/198 L75','Finite-dimensional irreducible holomorphic representations in the intended sl2 group example are symmetric powers; the named operator should satisfy theta^4=1.','Editorial corrections to missing propositions.','F','Symmetric-power classification for the simply connected SL2 group is standard, while descent depends on the chosen global group. The operator theta/phi and its normalization are absent, so the fourth-power identity cannot be checked.',['P15-MilneLAG'],confidence='low')
c(81,[110],'15/200 L108','The subgroup generated by elementary automorphisms of a Lie algebra is normal in its automorphism group.','Insertion stated without proof.','E','For elementary automorphisms exp(ad x) with ad x nilpotent, a exp(ad x) a^(-1)=exp(ad(a x)); conjugation preserves the generating set. Characteristic/definition restrictions on the exponential must be stated.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D18 gives the conjugation proof.')
c(82,[110],'15/200 L112','Elementary automorphisms act transitively on Cartan subalgebras.','Theorem wording corrected, proof elsewhere.','E','Milne8.17 proves this for finite-dimensional Lie algebras over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field, using elementary exponentials. For split Cartans in a split semisimple algebra,8.27 gives the relevant rational form. The unrestricted-field statement is not valid as written without context.',['P15-MilneLAG'])
c(83,[111],'15/200;202 L114;150–158','If h is Cartan in g and h⊂g′⊂g, then h is Cartan in g′; subalgebras containing a Cartan are self-normalizing, under the standard finite-dimensional characteristic-zero setup.','Proposed insertion with abbreviated proof reference.','E','The nested Cartan result is Milne8.4. The self-normalizer assertion follows from the Fitting decomposition for the Cartan action; the ambient hypotheses matter.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D18 gives the quotient/Fitting argument.')
c(84,[111],'15/200 L114','If g′⊂g has the same rank and contains a regular element of g, a subalgebra h⊂g′ is Cartan in g iff it is Cartan in g′.','Proposed corollary.','E','The assertion is established in the standard Cartan theory with rank defined through the minimum multiplicity of zero in ad(x), as in Milne8.21. This is not the alternative modern definition under which regular nilpotents in sl2 are called regular.',['P15-MilneLAG'])
c(85,[112],'15/200 L118','Cartan subalgebras are unchanged when a K-Lie algebra is regarded over a subfield k.','Stated as an insertion, no proof.','E','With Cartan defined as nilpotent and self-normalizing, the assertion follows algebraically: K·h is nilpotent; a proper subalgebra of a nilpotent Lie algebra has a strictly larger normalizer, forcing K·h=h. If one restricts the ambient theory to finite-dimensional k-algebras, K/k must also be finite.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D18 supplies the independent proof; literature search distinguished extension of scalars8.5 from this restriction assertion.')
c(86,[113],'15/202;204 L148;208','Near the identity of an analytic group, Ad(x) has eigenvalue1 with multiplicity at least rank(g); for connected real/complex G the inequality holds everywhere.','Statement continued across reused sheets; Lefschetz-principle lemma requested.','E','For characteristic-zero analytic groups, exp and the adjoint characteristic polynomial give the local bound. Over R or C the relevant analytic coefficient functions vanish on an open neighborhood and hence on the connected group. The exact missing Lefschetz lemma is not identified.',['P15-MilneLAG','P15-Gl16'],comp='computations.md §D19 gives the local exponential and analytic-continuation argument.')
c(87,[96],'15/202 L154','Rank is the least multiplicity of zero in the adjoint characteristic polynomial; for nilpotent g it equals dim(g); an absent rank formula must be verified.','Definition and example asserted; formula outside supplied sheet.','E','The rank convention and nilpotent example are established by Engel/Fitting theory and explicitly used in Milne8.6. The separate missing formula has no identifiable statement and is indexed as a reading/problem rather than invented.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D19: all ad(x) are nilpotent for nilpotent g.')
c(88,[97],'15/202 L154–160','The characteristic-free example involving sl2 is withdrawn; in characteristic2, sl2 is nilpotent.','Corrected by himself, with explicit counterexample.','E','The correction is correct: the diagonal basis element becomes the identity and central, while [e,f]=I. Any inference needing characteristic unequal2 must retain that restriction.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='C03, exact matrices over F2; full general bracket table proves the claim.')
c(89,[111],'15/202 L158','Regularity and equality of nilspaces should pass between g and a subalgebra described as the nilspace of a nilpotent; h is then asserted Cartan.','Proposed proposition with insufficient local hypotheses.','F','The named element/subalgebra and the role of h are not specified sufficiently. Read literally, the nilspace of an ad-nilpotent element is all g, which does not make arbitrary h Cartan. Standard regular-Fitting theorems supply possible reconstructions but do not decide the ink.',['P15-MilneLAG'],confidence='low')
c(90,[114],'15/204 L194','An equivariant bilinear map sends additive generalized weight spaces into the summed weight space, or multiplicative generalized eigenspaces into the product weight space.','Statement continued from an absent preceding sheet.','E','For compatible commuting operators and generalized eigenspaces, the assertion follows by binomial expansion of the induced tensor operator. For Lie weights, the action is D⊗1+1⊗D′; for a group element it is A⊗B.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D20 proves both nilpotence bounds.')
c(91,[98,105],'15/196;204 L33;198–202','Several numbered propositions are deleted or generalized; descent is to be moved to AlgebraVII and a non-algebraically-closed field allowed.','Withdrawals and editorial instructions referring to absent statements.','F','No exact surviving proposition is present to compare with a later theorem. Searches of generalized weights, Cartan theory and descent locate the subject but do not reconstruct propositions1–6 or their deleted hypotheses.',['P15-MilneLAG'],confidence='low')
c(92,[98],'15/204 L204','A sum in a missing proof should run over one root from each opposite pair.','Explicit correction to absent expression.','F','Opposite-root normalization can require a half-system, but the actual summand is not supplied. No numeric or sign correction can be certified from this sentence alone.',['P15-MilneLAG'],confidence='low')
c(93,[115],'15/204 L206','An operator written Ad(x) is claimed nilpotent under the hypotheses of a missing proposition.','Editorial replacement; hypotheses absent.','F','For a group element Ad(x) is invertible and cannot be nilpotent on a nonzero Lie algebra; ad(x) can be nilpotent. The notation may be an older convention or transcription issue, and the missing proposition prevents a final decision.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D20 gives the invertibility obstruction.',confidence='medium')
c(94,[106,107],'15/196 L37–49','Define a split semisimple pair, repair a referenced theorem3c, and choose root vectors by selecting one root from each pair {alpha,−alpha}.','Editorial instructions, partly determinate.','E','Split Cartan pairs and such opposite-root choices are standard; choosing X_alpha fixes the normalization of the opposite vector only after a bracket/pairing convention. The missing theorem3c and exact normalization remain unidentifiable.',['P15-MilneLAG'])
c(95,[116],'15/206 L235–243','Add infinite highest-weight modules W(lambda), Harish-Chandra formulas and invariant differential operators to the exposition.','Program for a future redactor.','C','Harish-Chandra’s centre theorem is established and was already attributed to him on the sheet. The later full proof opened in MIT lecture14 identifies one precise result; the sheet does not determine every intended formula or every module convention.',['P15-MIT-HC','P15-MilneLAG'])
c(96,[117],'15/206 L239–241','Exhibit triality automorphisms of split D4 and the fundamental representations of the classical split Lie algebras.','Expository task left to a redactor.','E','D4 diagram automorphisms and their lifts to the split simply connected/adjoint forms are established classical Lie theory; global-form restrictions matter. The manuscript asks for exposition, not a new existence conjecture.',['P15-MilneLAG'],confidence='medium')
c(97,[118],'15/206 L243','Cartier will supply an arithmetic treatment of integral structures for the split semisimple theory.','Assignment, no mathematical theorem stated.','F','The scope could include Chevalley lattices and divided powers, but no unique target statement or promised manuscript is identified. Rank-one formulas are independently checked; a general integral-form theorem is not silently substituted.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='C11 covers only the explicit rank-one correction.',confidence='low')
c(98,[119],'15/206 L249–251','Every nonempty open subset is dense in the intended polynomial/affine setting; dominance should use the Zariski topology.','Proposed insertion into a missing proposition.','E','The density assertion is exactly irreducibility, true for affine space over an infinite field in its usual Zariski sense or for the underlying scheme over a field. It is false for arbitrary reducible spaces, and finite rational-point topologies need care.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D21 gives an elementary polynomial proof and the reducible-space counterexample.')
c(99,[99],'15/210 L311–329','The roots of a complex semisimple Lie algebra form a reduced root system; all reduced root systems arise and classify the Lie algebra.','Stated, but root equation reads [h,x]=alpha(x).','E','The standard statement is established with [h,x]=alpha(h)x. As transcribed, alpha is not defined on x∈g and the equation is ill-typed. The surrounding classification cannot repair the ink silently.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D22 gives the type check; reading table records the proposed correction.')
c(100,[99],'15/210 L316–329','Automorphisms of the complex semisimple Lie algebra preserving a Cartan induce all root-system automorphisms; inner ones induce the Weyl group.','Stated without proof.','E','This is the standard split characteristic-zero automorphism/root-datum theorem. It distinguishes full diagram automorphisms from the Weyl action.',['P15-MilneLAG'])
c(101,[99],'15/210 L321–329','For a connected complex semisimple Lie group, the exponential lattice lies between2πi times the coroot and coweight lattices; the two quotient lattices describe centre and fundamental group.','Stated without proof.','E','The standard simply connected/adjoint classification gives these lattices and quotients. The dual character lattice records exactly which highest weights integrate to representations of the chosen global group.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D22 checks the SL2/PGL2 normalization as an explicit model.')
c(102,[99],'15/208;210 L272–279;330–333','Restricted roots for a maximal split torus or a maximal abelian subspace of the noncompact part form a possibly nonreduced root system, with Weyl group normalizer modulo centralizer.','Stated; one displayed root formula omits alpha.','E','The restricted-root theorem is established; the formula must read [a,x]=alpha(a)x. The quotient should be interpreted as the relative Weyl group/group scheme or its correct rational realization, not an unjustified equality of arbitrary rational point quotients.',['P15-MilneLAG'],confidence='medium')
c(103,[99],'15/208 L279–281','For a compact connected semisimple Lie group, torus characters occurring in the adjoint representation form a reduced root system and its character lattice lies between root and weight lattices.','Stated, apparently using real g in a complex eigenspace definition.','E','The standard assertion uses the complexification g_C for nonreal character eigenspaces. Taken literally in the real Lie algebra, multiplication by a complex character is not defined. The expected complexification is recorded as a reading alarm.',['P15-MilneLAG'],comp='computations.md §D22 distinguishes real compact and complex root spaces.')

c(104,[100],'15/216 L516–536','A local C^r group has a partially defined product on an open subset of G×G, a unit and inverses, with associativity whenever all four required pairs belong to that domain.','Definition; the fourth domain condition is ill-typed in the transcription.','E','The standard local-group definition has (x,m(y,z)) in the domain, not the element m(x,m(y,z)). Olver Definition2 records the exact four-pair condition. No globalization claim follows from these axioms alone.',['P15-Olver'],words='m(x,m(y,z)) appartiennent à Ω',comp='computations.md §D23 records the type check and domain restrictions.')
c(105,[100],'15/216 L538–542','The local inverse fixes e and is an involution.','Proved there, apart from an unclosed parenthesis.','E','The two identities follow from the stated unit, inverse and local associativity axioms; all four pairs needed for the displayed associativity are among the unit/inverse pairs.',['P15-Olver'],comp='computations.md §D23 gives the complete domain check.')
c(106,[100],'15/216 L548–553','A sufficiently differentiable local associative multiplication with a two-sided identity has a local inverse after shrinking the identity neighborhood.','Proof sketch invokes an earlier unavailable inverse-function lemma.','E','Bruhat1958, Chapter2 §2.2 Proposition1, proves the analytic version by the implicit-function theorem. The same local proof works for C^r, r≥1, in a finite-dimensional setting where that theorem holds. Arbitrary topological fields/manifolds need their analytic hypotheses.',['P15-Bruhat58'],comp='computations.md §D23 supplies the implicit-function and shrinking argument.')
c(107,[101],'15/214 L443–455','Earlier propositions3 and3bis are to extend to local group morphisms.','Left to the reader; the earlier propositions are not supplied.','F','The morphism definition is clear, but the propositions to be extended are absent. Searching local Lie-group treatments identifies the surrounding theory, not the missing exact assertions.',['P15-Olver','P15-Bruhat58'])
c(108,[101],'15/214 L457–461','Distributions supported at the identity of a local C^r group have an associative bilinear convolution whenever the required total differential order is at most r; for r=∞ this is an algebra.','Stated with the finite-r partial-product warning.','E','The germ of multiplication suffices for point-supported distributions. The smooth distribution/enveloping-algebra correspondence is classical; Beltiță–Nicolae Theorem5.2 supplies a modern precise operator formulation. The finite-r product is not a total algebra multiplication.',['P15-BN14','P15-Bruhat58'],comp='computations.md §D24 gives the convolution associativity and order bound.')
c(109,[101],'15/214 L463–464','A local group morphism induces a convolution-preserving map on distributions supported at the identity.','Stated without proof.','E','Pushforward commutes with tensor products and with the germ of multiplication; the result follows on every allowed differential order.',['P15-BN14'],comp='computations.md §D24 gives the exact pushforward identity.')
c(110,[101],'15/214 L466–470','The tangent bundle of a C^r local group is a C^(r−1) local group with multiplication Tm, inverse Tθ, identity0_e; its projection to G is a morphism.','Stated as easy to verify; the displayed tangent-domain notation has blanks.','E','The tangent functor preserves products, units and composition, so differentiating each local identity proves the claim. One derivative is lost for finite r. The missing domain should be TΩ⊂TG×TG.',['P15-Olver','P15-Bruhat58'],comp='computations.md §D24 gives the functorial verification.')
c(111,[101],'15/214 L462–472','The other results of sections12–16 and propositions11–12 should extend to local groups.','Left to the reader; antecedent statements are unavailable.','F','The individually stated tangent and convolution claims can be checked. The unspecified remainder cannot be identified from the supplied pages, and no source substitution establishes those exact missing statements.',['P15-Bruhat58','P15-BN14'])
c(112,[102],'15/218 L587–592','For an n-dimensional Lie group, left-invariant differential n-forms form a one-dimensional space, identified with the top exterior power of the cotangent space at e.','Proof sketch on the page.','E','Translation identifies an invariant form with its value at e. Li Example1.39 states the local-field version, and the manuscript’s wedge construction gives its elementary proof.',['P15-Li26','P15-MIT-Haar'],comp='computations.md §D25.')
c(113,[102],'15/218 L593–595','The coordinate coefficient of the invariant top form is det(D_2m(x^(-1),x)).','Displayed calculation.','E','The derivative of left translation by x^(-1) transports the form at x to the prescribed form at e. Bruhat Chapter7 §3.2 gives the corresponding coordinate Jacobian construction for the chosen side.',['P15-Bruhat58','P15-Li26'],comp='computations.md §D25 derives the Jacobian formula.')
c(114,[102],'15/218 L596–605','Right pullback on a left-invariant top form multiplies it by δ(y)=det(Ad(y))^(-1).','Displayed proof sketch.','E','Conjugation by y^(-1) computes this scalar at e. It is the inverse of the det-Ad convention used by several modern authors; the source action must be fixed before comparing symbols.',['P15-Li26','P15-MIT-Haar'],comp='C10 and computations.md §D25 check the ax+b group explicitly.')
c(115,[102],'15/218 L606–618','Over a local field, the absolute value of a nonzero invariant top form is Haar measure; its right-pullback modulus is |det Ad(y)|^(-1).','Stated with coordinate and modulus formulas.','E','Li Example1.39 and Proposition1.40 give the local-field measure construction and determinant formula, with the explicitly opposite modular-character convention of Remark1.24.',['P15-Li26'],comp='C10 and computations.md §D25; the field absolute value is the local module, including squared complex modulus when appropriate.')
c(116,[102,124],'15/226 L855–861','Every locally compact Hausdorff group has a nonzero positive left Haar measure, unique up to a positive scalar; likewise on the right.','Theorem with existence proof begun here and continued on reused sheets.','E','Haar/Weil theory predates these motivic pages. Li Theorem1.20 reproduces the covering-number argument with an explicit reference to Bourbaki Integration VII §1.2; Weil’s1935 seminar gives historical context under its stated countability setup.',['P15-Li26','P15-Weil35'],comp='computations.md §§D26–D27 reconstruct the proof, with the missing continuation clearly distinguished.')
c(117,[102,124],'15/226 L861–866','For compactly supported nonnegative functions, the covering number (f:g) is finite, left-translation invariant, positively homogeneous and subadditive in f.','Construction and elementary claims; first three called evident.','E','The finite covering of supp(f) by translates where g is bounded below proves finiteness; rescaling and joining covers give the identities and inequality. Li Theorem1.20 equations1.1–1.3 match.',['P15-Li26'],comp='computations.md §D26 proves each assertion; no finite numerical sample is used.')
c(118,[102,124],'15/224;226 L816;867–869','Covering numbers satisfy the supremum lower bound, (f:h)≤(f:g)(g:h), and positive uniform upper/lower bounds for normalized ratios.','Proved on the reused sheet.','E','Composition of finite covers proves submultiplicativity; applying it in both directions gives the ratio bounds. Li Theorem1.20 equations1.4–1.6 match the proof.',['P15-Li26'],comp='computations.md §D26; all denominators are positive for nonzero nonnegative compactly supported functions.')
c(119,[102,124],'15/224;226 L816–831;872','For g supported sufficiently close to e, the covering number becomes additive up to ε(h:g).','Proved on the page by a uniform-continuity argument.','E','The proof is the standard approximate-additivity step in the Haar construction, reproduced in Li Theorem1.20 equation1.7. The error coefficient has been independently recomputed.',['P15-Li26'],comp='C13 gives η=ε/(2(2C+ε)); computations.md §D26 proves the inequality without assuming attained infima.')
c(120,[102,124],'15/224 L833','An ultrafilter refining shrinking supports yields a normalized invariant positive functional from the covering-number ratios.','Ultrafilter chosen; the final limiting argument is not on this supplied sheet.','E','A single ultrafilter must be used simultaneously for every test function; compact ratio intervals give its limits, and approximate additivity makes the limit linear. Li Theorem1.20 completes this standard argument.',['P15-Li26'],comp='computations.md §D26 distinguishes the supplied beginning from the reconstructed completion.')
c(121,[102,124],'15/222 L757–765','Two Haar measures of the same side are proportional, via the auxiliary functions D_f and inversion.','End of a proof; definitions and first Fubini step occur on an unavailable preceding reused sheet.','E','Li Theorem1.20 contains the full matching D_f proof. Full support of the positive Haar measure turns equality against test functions into equality of continuous D_f; evaluation at e proves proportionality.',['P15-Li26'],comp='computations.md §D27 supplies the missing setup and verifies the shown final step.')
c(122,[102,124],'15/222 L767–768','Every left-invariant complex Radon measure is a complex scalar multiple of left Haar measure, and likewise on the right.','Corollary stated without proof.','E','The same Fubini argument works with the second measure complex; conjugating the continuous difference replaces the square used in the real proof. The scalar can be zero. This concerns Radon measures, not arbitrary finitely additive functionals.',['P15-Li26'],comp='computations.md §D27 gives the complex-valued modification explicitly.')
c(123,[102,124],'15/222 L770–790','Lebesgue measure on R, dx/x on positive reals, and the integral over any period interval on R/Z are Haar measures.','Examples with change-of-variables computations.','E','Translation, dilation and periodicity verify the three invariances. These standard examples appear in Haar theory well before the manuscript.',['P15-Li26','P15-Bruhat58'],comp='computations.md §D28 redoes all three substitutions.')
c(124,[102,124],'15/222 L792–793','A necessary and sufficient condition for discreteness is announced, but its condition is on the missing continuation.','Statement cut off after il faut et il suffit que.','F','The standard theorem is μ({e})>0 iff G is discrete (Li Proposition1.21), but that cannot be substituted for the absent manuscript wording, which might use an equivalent cutoff formulation.',['P15-Li26'])
c(125,[102,124],'15/220 L679–692','For a unimodular group Haar measure is invariant under both translations and inversion, with the corresponding formulas for integrable functions and measurable sets.','Stated formulas.','E','Inversion exchanges left and right Haar measures; its positive scalar is1 because inversion squared is identity. The integral and set formulas follow by change of variables.',['P15-Li26','P15-Bruhat58'],comp='computations.md §D28 checks normalization and inversion.')
c(126,[102,124],'15/220 L694–698','A locally compact group with a compact identity neighborhood invariant under all inner automorphisms is unimodular.','Proved on the page.','E','That neighborhood has finite positive Haar mass; inner conjugation scales it by the modular character, forcing the scalar to1.',['P15-Li26'],comp='computations.md §D28 reproduces the one-line proof and the finite-positive-mass hypothesis.')
c(127,[102,124],'15/220 L702–711','Discrete, compact and abelian groups are unimodular; counting normalization and total-mass-one normalization differ for a nontrivial finite group.','Corollary with explanation.','E','Counting measure is bi-invariant; the modular character of a compact group has compact image in R_{>0}, hence is trivial; conjugation is trivial in the abelian case. For a finite group the two normalizations differ by its order.',['P15-Li26','P15-Bruhat58'],comp='computations.md §D28.')
c(128,[102,124],'15/220 L713–714','A subgroup or quotient of a unimodular group need not be unimodular.','Stated with an exercise reference absent from the package.','E','The connected solvable group R⋉R² with weights1 and−1 is unimodular; its subgroup and quotient with only the weight1 direction are ax+b and nonunimodular. This verifies both quantifiers, including a closed normal subgroup for the quotient.',['P15-Li26'],comp='C16 and computations.md §D28 give the explicit group laws and determinants.')
c(129,[102,124],'15/220 L716–717','Connected semisimple and connected nilpotent Lie groups are unimodular.','Explicitly deferred to later.','E','For real/complex Lie groups, trace(ad X)=0 in both cases; det Ad(exp X)=1, and connectedness gives the result globally. Bruhat1958 Chapter7 §3.1 explicitly proves the semisimple case.',['P15-Bruhat58','P15-Li26'],comp='computations.md §D28 proves both cases and retains connectedness.')
c(130,[102,124],'15/228 L885–911','Relative invariance under a right action gives the reciprocal integral multiplier; inversion sends a measure to its pushforward and exchanges left with right invariance.','Definitions and change-of-variables identities.','E','Pushforward and the group-opposite convention give the formulas directly. Their χ and Δ signs depend on whether x↦xs or x↦xs^(-1) is the defined action.',['P15-Li26','P15-Bruhat58'],comp='computations.md §D28 derives the set and test-function formulas from the stated right action.')
c(131,[103],'18/16 L1239–1245','For finite free formal disks over a commutative ring, take distributions to be the filtered union of the finite-order duals and coordinate functions to be their continuous completed-power-series dual. A filtered linear map of these distribution coalgebras arises uniquely from a pointed formal map exactly when it preserves the diagonal and carries the degree-zero line onto the degree-zero line. The broader manuscript category of formal kernels is not reconstructed from missing earlier definitions.','Proposition with proof reduced to formal charts.','B','For finite free formal disks the coefficient argument proves the claim over a commutative ring, with the onto condition supplying the needed unit. Strickland Proposition4.59 and Remark4.62 give a precise coalgebra/formal-scheme duality under a good-basis hypothesis. The full unspecified infinite-chart category is not silently identified with that theorem.',['P15-Strickland00'],comp='C07 and computations.md §D29 give the finite-free proof and separate the ring hypothesis from the printed nonzero shorthand.',confidence='medium')
c(132,[103],'18/16 L1247–1255','Diagonal compatibility is equivalent in the displayed bases to a_{α,β1+β2}=Σ_{α1+α2=α}a_{α1,β1}a_{α2,β2}.','Displayed calculation; the manuscript stops before the reconstructed power-series argument.','E','Transposing the diagonal makes multiplication of coordinate power series; equating coefficients yields exactly this convolution identity. For finite free disks it reconstructs the substitution homomorphism from the series for coordinate generators.',['P15-Strickland00'],comp='computations.md §D29 checks both sides and the degree-zero counit.')
c(133,[103],'18/16 L1236–1237','In finite free formal coordinates over a commutative ring K, formal N-valued series are Hom_K(U(V),N), and pointed series are the maps vanishing on U0(V). The manuscript uses this to define formal maps to a separated module N and asserts agreement with the earlier definition in a free chart.','Construction; its opening sentence is on18/018 L9 in the other supplied package.','C','The completed-power-series/finite-distribution pairing verifies the finite free statement. The other package supplies the filtered coalgebra, formal charts and morphism definitions at18/018 L5–9 and18/022 L83–93. Its references to earlier polynomial-map definitions still leave infinite-free and separated-module conventions requiring an additional check.',['P15-Strickland00'],comp='computations.md §D29 gives the finite free pairing and records the supplied cross-package continuation.',confidence='medium')

c(134,[33],'15/221 L722–726','Every countable k, with k0 its algebraic part, can be exhausted by finitely generated k0-algebras whose transition morphisms are smooth with geometrically connected fibres.','Asserted without construction.','C','False in this generality: take k to be an algebraic closure of Qbar(t), k0=Qbar. Smooth geometrically connected generic fibres force each fraction field K_i to be relatively algebraically closed in the union k, hence algebraically closed, impossible for a positive-transcendence-degree finitely generated extension of Qbar. EGA finite spreading does not give this tower. Bakker–Tsimerman2025 Lemmas4.12–4.13 give a related path/Betti equivalence for finitely generated regular extensions, with different hypotheses.',['P15-EGAIV3','P15-BT25'],comp='Independent proof in computations.md §D8; refined conditional inverse-limit statement CL-SA07-032. No finite test is used.',words='on peut supposer les morphismes de transition ... lisses à fibres géom. connexes')

c(135,[60],'18/11 L1134','For a finite-dimensional pure Betti–de Rham realization over k⊂C with Hodge filtration defined over k, let alpha be its comparison matrix in rational Betti and k-rational de Rham bases, and beta the canonical Hodge splitting into the associated graded filtration. Then k(alpha,beta) is contained in the compositum Q(k(alpha),conjugate(k(alpha))). This verifies the manuscript inclusion in this specified pure setting.','Displayed inclusion without proof; the full realization category is not restated here.','B','The standard pure Hodge decomposition as the intersection of opposite filtrations gives the inclusion by linear algebra. Deligne82 Theorem1.4 and the Hodge-structure discussion supply the geometric realization hypotheses. No period algebraic-independence conjecture is needed; arbitrary choices of splittings are not covered.',['P15-D82'],comp='computations.md §D30 gives the conjugation matrix and kernel computation proving the field inclusion.',words='K ⊂ Q(k(alpha),overline{k(alpha)})')
c(136,[60],'18/11 L1137','For a tensor Betti–de Rham comparison alpha over k⊂C on a category containing Q(-n), its coordinate field k(alpha) contains (2pi i)^n, using compatible Tate bases; changing nonzero bases over k does not change this field inclusion.','Displayed formula without proof.','B','The Tate comparison is multiplication by the corresponding power of2pi i, or its reciprocal under the dual convention. Evaluation of the tensor comparison on the Tate object is defined over k, so the power belongs to the comparison field. Deligne82 §1 gives the chosen Betti and de Rham Tate objects explicitly.',['P15-D82'],comp='computations.md §D30; a field contains a nonzero scalar iff it contains its reciprocal.',words='(2i pi)^n ∈ k(alpha) si Q(-n) ∈ Ob M')

