C[21]['refs']=['P15-DM82','P15-Tate66','P15-Faltings83']
C[25]['statement']='Manuscript programme: recover geometric Hodge variation from one fibre and monodromy. Precise verified special case: for a smooth proper algebraic morphism f:X→S with S reduced, connected, separated and of finite type over C, a global flat section of R^nf_*C whose value has Hodge type(p,q) at one point has that type everywhere. For abelian schemes A,B/S, a global morphism of integral R¹ local systems that is induced by an algebraic morphism at one fibre comes from a unique morphism of abelian schemes. An arbitrary local variation on an analytic disc is outside these hypotheses.'
C[25]['class']='C'
C[26]['statement']='Manuscript assertion for polarized rigidified motivic moduli; verified reconstruction restricted to a fine moduli space of polarized abelian varieties with sufficient level: over a reduced connected finite-type algebraic C-base S, a specified global integral H¹ local-system isomorphism preserving the polarization and level data, and induced by a fibre isomorphism at one point, identifies the two families. Based monodromy plus a compatible fibre identification can supply that global local-system map. No arbitrary analytic-base or arbitrary-motive moduli theorem is asserted.'
C[45]['statement']='For p-divisible groups up to isogeny over a complete discretely valued characteristic-zero field with perfect residue field, characterize the filtered Frobenius realization. A relevant precise later theorem concerns rational representations: weakly admissible filtered(phi,N)-modules are admissible in the semistable category; N=0 is the crystalline subcategory. The p-divisible case additionally requires the two Hodge–Tate weights0 and1 (or their negatives under the dual convention), and integral models require lattice data. Those extra p-divisible assertions are not proved by the cited general admissibility theorem alone.'
C[45]['class']='C'
C[50]['statement']='Let π be profinite (or compact Hausdorff), and u:π→G(Q_p) a continuous homomorphism into a finite-dimensional p-adic linear algebraic group. Then π/keru, with its quotient topology, is homeomorphic to the compact closed image u(π), hence is a p-adic analytic group. The unqualified assertion for arbitrary sources or nonclosed images is not adopted.'
C[81]['statement']='For a finite-dimensional Lie algebra over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field, elementary automorphisms generated by the appropriate nilpotent adjoint exponentials act transitively on Cartan subalgebras. For split Cartans in a split semisimple algebra, the separately cited rational split theorem applies. No conjugacy of all Cartans over an arbitrary field is asserted.'
C[84]['statement']='Let K/k be a finite extension of characteristic-zero fields and g a finite-dimensional K-Lie algebra, regarded also as a finite-dimensional k-Lie algebra. With Cartan meaning nilpotent and self-normalizing, its Cartan subalgebras are unchanged: any k-Cartan is automatically K-linear. The independent proof also explains the algebraic extension beyond this finite-dimensional scope.'
C[47]['class']='C'
C[47]['fate']='The printed equality does not follow from the stated centralization condition: distinct homomorphic sections of a split extension give a counterexample (C05). Equality requires an additional normalization or vanishing of the difference character. This is an elementary group-theoretic correction, not an unidentified claim.'
C[47]['present_status']='As of 2026-11-19: '+C[47]['fate']
